Advertising Channel Performance & Spend Efficiency Dashboard
1. Purpose
This dashboard shows how advertising spend across different channels translates into product sales and efficiency.
It is built in Power BI on top of a long-format dataset (one row per channel/spend/sales record).

It answers these questions:

How much are we spending in total, and how many products are we selling?
Which channels get the most spend and the most sales?
Which channels are most efficient (sales per unit of spend)?
How is total spend distributed across channels?
2. Data Structure
Source table

File: Advertising_Data_Long.csv (imported into Power BI as table Advertising_Long)
Columns

Channel (text) Example: TV, Billboards, Google_Ads, etc.
Spend (numeric) Advertising cost for that record.
Product_Sold (numeric) Number of products sold associated with that spend.
Assumptions

Numeric fields (Spend, Product_Sold) are cleaned and stored as numbers.
Each row is an observation for a given channel and spend amount.
No date/region dimensions are included in this first version.
3. Measures (DAX)
All measures are created in the Advertising_Long table.

 Download
 Copy
Total Spend =
    SUM(Advertising_Long[Spend])

Total Product Sold =
    SUM(Advertising_Long[Product_Sold])

Average Product Sold =
    AVERAGE(Advertising_Long[Product_Sold])

Sales per Spend =
    DIVIDE(
        [Total Product Sold],
        [Total Spend]
    )
Interpretation

Total Spend: Overall advertising investment.
Total Product Sold: Overall units sold.
Sales per Spend: Efficiency metric (how many products per 1 unit of currency spent). Higher is better.
4. Dashboard Structure
4.1 Page Name
Page title:
Advertising Channel Performance & Spend Efficiency

4.2 Top KPIs (Cards)
Visual type: Card

Card: Total Spend

Field: [Total Spend]
Format: Currency or whole number.
Card: Total Product Sold

Field: [Total Product Sold]
Card: Sales per Spend

Field: [Sales per Spend]
Format: Decimal (2–3 places).
These give a quick “at a glance” view of overall performance.

4.3 Total Spend and Sales by Channel
Visual type: Clustered column chart

Axis: Channel
Values:
[Total Spend]
[Total Product Sold]
Purpose
Compare how much is spent per channel vs how many products are sold.

Notes

Turn on data labels.
If spend and sales are on very different scales, use a secondary axis or split into two separate visuals.
4.4 Efficiency by Channel (Sales per Spend)
Visual type: Column or bar chart

Axis: Channel
Values: [Sales per Spend]
Purpose
Show which channels give the most sales per unit of spend.

Notes

Sort the visual by [Sales per Spend] descending to highlight the best channels at the top.
4.5 Spend vs Product Sold (Optional Scatter)
Visual type: Scatter chart (optional)

X-Axis: Spend (Sum)
Y-Axis: Product_Sold (Sum)
Legend: Channel
Details: leave blank to have one point per channel (if you aggregate first) or use raw data.
Purpose
See the relationship between spend and sales; detect outliers and non-linear behavior.

4.6 Donut Chart: Share of Spend by Channel
Visual type: Donut chart

Legend: Channel
Values: [Total Spend]
Purpose
Show the percentage of total advertising budget each channel receives.

Formatting

Title: Share of Spend by Channel
Data labels: Category + Percent of total.
Keep colors consistent with other visuals (e.g., TV is always same color across all charts).
5. Naming & Style Guidelines
Table name: Advertising_Long
Measure names: Use readable names with spaces Example: Total Spend, Total Product Sold, Sales per Spend
Visual titles (examples):
Total Spend and Sales by Channel
Channel Efficiency (Sales per Spend)
Spend vs Product Sold by Channel
Share of Spend by Channel
Keep fonts and colors consistent across visuals so the dashboard feels cohesive.

6. How to Use the Dashboard
Read the top cards first to understand overall spend, sales, and efficiency.
Use “Total Spend and Sales by Channel” to see which channels are biggest in both spend and output.
Use “Efficiency by Channel” to see which channels give you the most return per unit of spend.
Use the donut chart to check whether your budget allocation makes sense relative to efficiency.
If added, use slicers (Channel/Date/Region, once available) to filter all visuals.
